-- Q12305: Statement of Cash Flows [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 230-10-45-27A, cash flows from derivatives designated as hedges follow the classification of the hedged item''s cash flows. Since interest payments on variable-rate debt are operating activities, the swap settlement payments are also classified as operating.
Wrong (A): There is no default rule classifying all derivative settlements as operating. The classification depends on the hedged item or, for undesignated derivatives, on the nature of the cash flow.
Wrong (B): While the underlying debt is a financing instrument, the hedged cash flows are interest payments, which are operating under U.S. GAAP.
Wrong (D): Derivative instruments are not automatically classified as investing. The hedge designation and hedged item determine classification under ASC 230.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12305;

-- Q12306: Statement of Cash Flows [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 230, pension expense is a noncash accrual that reduced net income, while the $250,000 contribution is the actual cash outflow. The accrued pension liability increased by $70,000 ($320,000 expense minus $250,000 paid), and this operating liability increase is added to net income under the indirect method.
Wrong (A): While the gross mechanics involve adding back expense and deducting contributions, the net presentation shows a single $70,000 add-back through the liability change.
Wrong (C): Subtracting $70,000 reverses the correct direction. When expense exceeds contributions, the liability increases, which is an add-back, not a subtraction.
Wrong (D): Pension expense and cash contributions rarely equal each other. The $70,000 difference must be reconciled through the operating activities adjustment.$EXPL$
